\begin{abstract}
Decentralized multi-robot task-allocation (MRTA) methods are often evaluated in simulation without accounting for execution constraints on embedded edge hardware. This paper introduces a uniform, allocator-independent, value-ranked Top-\(K\) candidate-restriction mechanism and evaluates it across six decentralized allocators. Clue-Informed Probabilistic Search (CLIPS) serves as the primary study, with Collaborative Visit (CV) providing complementary cross-mission evaluation. Paired AGX Orin simulations quantify mission-performance and computation tradeoffs, RP2040 hardware-in-the-loop trials characterize embedded feasibility, and selected CLIPS operating points are validated on a physical four-robot testbed. At 10\% candidate retention in CLIPS, mean AGX computation per call was reduced by approximately 26--99.6\% across algorithms, with an 8.9\% mean paired maximum-step degradation. Candidate restriction also moved HIPC and DMCHBA from infeasible to feasible execution under the imposed 30-s RP2040 threshold, while DGA remained infeasible across the evaluated CLIPS conditions. CV exhibited similar computational benefits but different allocator-specific tradeoffs, demonstrating that neither retained percentage nor absolute \(K\) alone predicts deployment behavior. Physical trials remained highly concordant with simulation (\(\mathrm{CCC}=0.987\)) with a 2.44-step mean penalty, while sensitivity tests showed broadly stable restriction-response trends across the tested grid sizes, team sizes, and communication conditions. These results characterize candidate restriction as an effective but allocator-, mission-, and hardware-dependent control for embedded MRTA deployment.
\end{abstract}

