\section{Introduction}

Multi-robot systems (MRS) have a multitude of potential and realized real-world applications in domains such as logistics, surveillance, and search and rescue \cite{chakraa2023optimization}. The performance of an MRS is heavily dependent upon an effective task allocator. The goal of Multi-robot task allocation (MRTA) is to distribute tasks among autonomous robots to optimize a mission objective \cite{athira2024systematic}. MRTA methods are commonly categorized as centralized or decentralized: centralized methods use globally aggregated information or a central decision point, whereas decentralized methods coordinate through peer-to-peer communication \cite{chakraa2023optimization}. This paper is restricted to decentralized methods due to their scalability and fault tolerance\cite{Almadhoun2019}, key attributes in communication-degraded or large-scale multi-robot environments where the resource-constrained edge devices considered in this study could be expected to operate.

Despite extensive MRS literature, recent surveys identify a persistent simulation-to-deployment gap\cite{athira2024systematic,chakraa2023optimization,Almadhoun2019}. This gap is reflected in limited real-world experimentation, heavy reliance on simulation-only evaluation, and inadequate consideration of computational and communication constraints. Recent benchmarking under degraded communications has addressed this gap by evaluating algorithm behavior under deployment-relevant communication impairment \cite{mybenchmark}. However, practical execution on edge devices, where allocation latency can affect mission time, remains less understood. In addition, to the authors' knowledge, no cross-family evaluation of hardware execution on embedded platforms currently exists. 

Prior work has addressed the high computational complexity of MRTA algorithms by reducing allocation scope. Recent methods include utility sparsification, task-subset selection, and robot-task decomposition \cite{liu2011multilevel,xiong2023selective,martin2023clustering}. These mechanisms are generally targeted at, or integrated with, a particular allocator or application, leaving limited evidence that a restriction mechanism transfers across algorithm families, missions, and hardware. In this study, allocation scope is reduced through an allocator-independent, value-ranked Top-$K$ candidate-restriction mechanism. We evaluate six prominent MRTA algorithms in Clue-Informed Probabilistic Search \cite{mypaper1} and Collaborative Visit \cite{2020benchmark} missions on two edge-computing platforms. We offer the following contributions:
We offer the following contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A uniform, allocator-independent, value-ranked Top-\(K\)
    candidate-restriction mechanism evaluated across six decentralized
    allocators and two mission structures.
    
    \item Direct measurement and decomposition of candidate-restriction and
    allocator latency on AGX Orin and RP2040 platforms, including
    allocator-specific hardware-feasible operating regions.
    
    \item A paired performance--compute characterization with per-allocator
    Pareto operating-point selection, HIL execution, and physical four-robot
    testbed validation.
    
    \item Evidence that useful candidate limits are allocator-, mission-, and
    hardware-dependent, supported by cross-mission and environmental-sensitivity
    analyses.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}

Computational scalability in multi-robot task allocation (MRTA) has been addressed by reducing different parts of the allocation pipeline. Selective Multi-Robot Task Planning optimizes a fixed subset of statistically critical tasks while assigning the remainder through simpler policies \cite{xiong2023selective}; Li et al.'s ``top $k\%$'' method instead assigns groups of nearby deposition paths iteratively until all paths are allocated \cite{li2024deposition}. Other approaches reduce problem coupling or defer computation: Martin et al. cluster robots and tasks into smaller allocation subproblems \cite{martin2023clustering}, Liu et al. adapt the number of arriving warehouse tasks accumulated before optimization \cite{liu2022adaptive}, and Dahlquist et al. generate candidate bundles using inexpensive estimates before applying high-fidelity path evaluation only to the most promising candidates \cite{dahlquist2026varying}. These studies establish the broader quality--computation tradeoff, but they intervene at different stages (task selection, decomposition, batching, or cost evaluation), are focused on centralized allocator methods, and each is integrated with a particular allocator or application.

The closest precedent to the present work is the sparse assignment method proposed by Liu and Shell \cite{liu2011multilevel}. Their method aggregates a global robot--task utility matrix, retains a fraction $\rho$ of the highest-valued entries in each robot's row, and uses hypergraph partitioning to form smaller one-to-one assignment problems that local leaders solve using the Hungarian algorithm. Candidate restriction is therefore coupled to a new hybrid centralized--distributed solver and its decomposition structure rather than applied as a standalone preprocessor to existing allocators. Their simulations dispatched homogeneous robots to destination waypoints and evaluated total assignment distance relative to Hungarian and greedy baselines. Computational benefits were characterized primarily through matrix sparsity, parallel decomposition, and complexity analysis rather than measured embedded execution latency or closed-loop mission outcomes. 

In contrast, the present work applies a common value-ranked Top-$K$ candidate restriction before the six existing MRTA algorithms without partitioning the robot--task graph or modifying allocator internals, including algorithms with single-task and bundle-forming allocation structures. We evaluate both mission-level performance and measured allocator compute latency in two established mission types on edge and resource-constrained hardware. The contribution to the field is therefore a standardized cross-family characterization of when candidate restriction transfers across algorithms, missions, and hardware, rather than the introduction of candidate reduction itself.

\section{System Design and Candidate Restriction}
\label{sec:system_design}

\subsection{System Architecture and Missions}

This study evaluates value-ranked candidate-set restriction within a common decentralized multi-robot architecture. Four robots operate on a discrete $19\times19$ grid. Each MRTA algorithm is implemented as an allocator that selects a task cell, while a shared robot layer handles motion execution, A* path planning, sensing, local mission-state updates, communication, collision avoidance, and metric collection. Simulation execution is event-driven and asynchronous. Full implementation details for the simulation architecture and allocation algorithms can be accessed at \cite{mybenchmark}.

Two mission types are considered. In Clue-Informed Probabilistic Search (CLIPS)\cite{mypaper1,mybenchmark}, agents search for a hidden target using information derived from discovered clues. Robots initially execute a common deterministic search sweep, then, upon finding a clue, local belief maps are updated to their posterior distributions. At each allocation event, the unrestricted candidate set contains all valid cells that the robot locally considers unsearched. In Collaborative Visit (CV) \cite{2020benchmark,mybenchmark}, the environment contains known target locations that must be visited by the team. Unlike CLIPS, the first allocation event is at robot start positions. The task set contains all target locations locally considered to be unvisited by the team. 

\subsection{Value-Ranked Candidate-Set Restriction}

Let $\mathcal{C}_{i,t}$ denote the set of locally valid candidate tasks available to robot $i$ at allocation event $t$. Candidate restriction retains the first $K$ elements after lexicographic ranking:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C}_{i,t}^{K}
=
\operatorname{first}_{K}
\left[
\operatorname{sort}_{q\in\mathcal{C}_{i,t}}
\left(
-P_i(q,t),
d_1(x_i(t),q),
q_x,
q_y
\right)
\right],
\label{eq:topk_filter}
\end{equation}

where $P_i(q,t)$ is the locally maintained value of candidate $q$, $d_1(x_i(t),q)$ is the Manhattan distance from the robot's current cell, and $(q_x,q_y)$ provides deterministic tie-breaking. Candidates with higher local value are therefore retained first. In CV, all active targets have equal value, so distance is the effective primary ranking criterion.

The retained count is fixed before each trial from the full problem size. CLIPS uses a basis of 361 grid cells, while CV uses a basis of 50 initial targets. The value of $K$ is not recomputed as tasks are completed. When the number of valid candidates is less than or equal to $K$, the complete valid set is retained.

For consistent presentation across the analysis, each retained-candidate condition is assigned a restriction mode as defined in Table~\ref{tab:restriction_modes}. R0 denotes the unrestricted candidate condition, and increasing mode indices indicate progressively stronger restriction. Because the two missions begin with different candidate-set sizes, the retained percentage and absolute value of \(K\) are reported separately for each mission.

