Candidate restriction is applied only when an allocator requests candidates during allocation. Existing valid commitments are handled through each allocator's normal repair, commitment, and deconfliction rules. Thus, the restriction changes the set of candidates admitted to the allocation process without replacing the internal decision mechanism of the allocator.

\subsection{Decentralized Allocation Methods}

The study evaluates six decentralized allocators spanning single-task auctions, bundle and path methods, implicit team planning, deterministic assignment, and evolutionary optimization. The implementations preserve the algorithm-specific decision semantics presented in \cite{mybenchmark}. Defining algorithmic characteristics needed to interpret responses to candidate restriction are summarized here.

\textbf{CBAA:}
The Consensus-Based Auction Algorithm \cite{CBAA} maintains one current task per robot. Each robot bids for an immediate task and exchanges winning claims with its peers until conflicts are resolved.

\textbf{ACBBA:}
The Asynchronous Consensus-Based Bundle Algorithm \cite{ACBBA} constructs an ordered task bundle using marginal insertion bids. Timestamped consensus reconciles asynchronous claims, and losing a task invalidates the dependent suffix of the local bundle.

\textbf{PI:}
The Performance Impact algorithm \cite{PI} evaluates tasks using their marginal effect on route cost. Robots exchange task ownership and significance information while maintaining ordered paths. When a task is lost, only that task is removed and the remaining path is reevaluated.

\textbf{HIPC:}
The Hybrid Information and Plan Consensus algorithm \cite{HIPC} predicts the behavior of locally known peers and greedily constructs a local team plan. Each robot retains its assigned portion and exchanges task-level claims to resolve conflicts.

\textbf{DMCHBA:}
The Distributed Matching-by-Clone Hungarian-Based Algorithm \cite{DMCHBA} creates agent clones and applies the Hungarian method to the current candidate set. Tasks assigned to the local robot's clones are ordered into a route, from which a bounded prefix is committed.

\textbf{DGA:}
The Decentralized Genetic Algorithm \cite{DGA} evolves team-wide assignments of the current candidate set through selection, crossover, mutation, and repair. Plans are evaluated using a makespan-dominant objective with a secondary total-route-cost term.

Multi-task algorithms use a common planning horizon of three. Because this study evaluates each algorithm relative to its own unrestricted condition, holding planning depth constant supports controlled intra-algorithm comparison across candidate-set sizes.


\section{Experimental Methodology}
\label{sec:experimental_methodology}

\subsection{Experimental Campaign}

The primary campaigns use a \(19\times19\) grid, four robots, and ideal
communication. The CLIPS simulation campaign contains 500 paired scenarios
for each algorithm and restriction setting. CV serves as a complementary
cross-mission evaluation and contains 100 paired 50-target scenarios per
algorithm and setting. Within each mission, the same scenario instances are
reused across algorithms and candidate levels for paired-scenario evaluation.
Separate CLIPS sensitivity campaigns vary grid size, team size, and
communication condition over R1--R5.

Performance and timing summaries use completed trials, while non-completions
are retained in the failure accounting. Paired changes are calculated only
from scenario IDs completed under both the restricted and corresponding R0
conditions.

\subsection{Embedded Hardware and HIL Execution}

The evaluated processors are edge-computing platforms used as robotic control systems. The RP2040 processors are embedded in Pololu 3pi+ robots used in the physical testbed and represent an extremely resource-constrained edge platform for task-allocation execution. The NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin was selected for its common use in larger robotic platforms and higher computational performance.

The AGX Orin experiments use CPU affinity with single-threaded workers and single-threaded numerical-library execution. Multiple conditions may be evaluated concurrently, but each simulator worker executes one trial stream on a dedicated processor core.

The hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) architecture retains scenario execution, simulated motion, sensing, communication delivery, path planning, and event scheduling on the host. At each allocation event, the current robot state and delivered allocator information are transferred to an RP2040. The RP2040 executes both candidate restriction and task allocation, measures the complete allocator call locally, and returns the selected task, outgoing allocator messages, and updated allocator state before the host advances the mission. All reported HIL allocation decisions and timing measurements therefore originate from RP2040 execution. The AGX Orin and RP2040 use the same allocator implementation. The exact implementation differs from \cite{mybenchmark} only through optimizations required by the memory constraints of the RP2040.

A maximum combined allocator-call time of 30~s is imposed as an extreme practical execution threshold. A condition that reproducibly exceeds this limit is classified as hardware-infeasible under the imposed requirement.

\subsection{Physical Testbed Mode Selection}
\label{sec:physical_mode_selection}

Physical-validation modes were selected separately for each allocator using the CLIPS HIL results. A restriction mode was considered hardware-feasible only if all 25 trials completed without failure and every combined allocator call remained below the 30-s execution threshold. Among the feasible modes, Pareto-dominated conditions were removed using mean maximum robot steps and mean combined allocator time per call, with lower values preferred for both metrics. A mode was Pareto dominated when another feasible mode was no worse in either metric and strictly better in at least one.

For the remaining Pareto-efficient modes, mean maximum steps and mean
combined allocator time per call were independently min--max normalized to
$[0,1]$ within each algorithm. The selected mode minimized the equal-weight
distance from the ideal performance--compute point,

\begin{equation}
J_{a,r}
=
\sqrt{
\frac{1}{2}\widetilde{S}_{a,r}^{\,2}
+
\frac{1}{2}\widetilde{C}_{a,r}^{\,2}},
\end{equation}

where $\widetilde{S}_{a,r}$ and $\widetilde{C}_{a,r}$ are the normalized
mean maximum-step count and normalized mean combined allocator time per call
for algorithm $a$ at restriction mode $r$. The feasible Pareto-efficient mode
with the smallest $J_{a,r}$ was selected for physical validation.

\subsection{Metrics}

Mission performance is evaluated using the maximum number of steps completed
by any robot in the team,

\begin{equation}
    S_{\max}=\max_{i\in\mathcal{R}} S_i,
    \label{eq:max_steps}
\end{equation}

where \(S_i\) is the number of completed grid-cell transitions by robot \(i\).
This metric serves as a discrete proxy for motion-based mission completion time
under constant movement speed \cite{2020benchmark,mybenchmark}. Total team
steps are additionally used as a secondary diagnostic of aggregate team effort
when interpreting algorithm-specific behavior.

Computational cost is measured using mean combined candidate-restriction and
allocator time per call. The timed region includes candidate discovery,
validity checking, value and distance evaluation, candidate ranking or
selection, truncation, returned-set construction, and subsequent allocator
execution. Movement, sensing, path planning, communication transport, host
waiting, and result serialization are excluded. Candidate-restriction overhead
is reported as its aggregate share of combined timed execution, calculated as
the total candidate-restriction time divided by total combined time across
completed trials in each condition.

For either primary metric \(X\), the paired change for trial \(i\) and
restriction mode \(r\), relative to the unrestricted R0 condition, is

\begin{equation}
    \Delta X_{i,r}
    =
    100
    \frac{X_{i,r}-X_{i,\mathrm{R0}}}
         {X_{i,\mathrm{R0}}}.
    \label{eq:paired_metric_change}
\end{equation}

Positive values indicate an increase relative to R0, corresponding to
additional maximum robot steps for mission performance or increased
computation time for computational cost; negative values indicate a reduction.


\subsection{Statistical Analysis}

Statistical analyses preserve the paired-scenario structure where applicable.
Simulator--HIL and simulator--physical validation use paired $t$-tests to
quantify systematic differences in maximum robot steps, two one-sided tests
(TOST) to evaluate practical equivalence \cite{schuirmann1987}, and Lin's
concordance correlation coefficient (CCC) to quantify agreement
\cite{lin1989}. Equivalence margins of $\pm1$ step for HIL validation and
$\pm4$ steps for physical validation are used.

Environmental sensitivity is evaluated using permutation-based omnibus tests
on five-mode restriction-response curves spanning R1--R5. Team-size and
communication comparisons preserve matched trial IDs and permute environment
labels within trials, whereas grid-size comparisons are treated as independent
and permute environment labels across complete curves. Each test uses 99,999
permutations, with permutation $p$-values calculated using the finite-sampling
correction $(b+1)/(B+1)$ \cite{phipson2010}. Omnibus tests are Holm corrected
across allocators within each environmental family and metric
\cite{holm1979}. Only significant omnibus results proceed to pairwise
environment comparisons and restriction-level follow-ups, with Holm correction
applied within each subsequent comparison family.

Cross-mission computational effects are evaluated using linear mixed-effects
models of log-transformed mean combined allocator time per call. Candidate-set
size, mission, allocator, and their interactions are treated as fixed effects,
with random intercepts accounting for scenario and scenario--allocator
variation. Statistical significance is evaluated at $\alpha=0.05$.


