Figure~\ref{fig:performance} shows that sensitivity to candidate restriction
depends on both mission type and allocator. Because restriction modes are
displayed at equal horizontal spacing, the trajectories should not be
interpreted as responses to equally spaced changes in candidate retention.

The unrestricted results provide context for the paired degradation curves.
In CLIPS, mean maximum steps at R0 ranged from \(41.2\) to \(42.5\) for CBAA,
ACBBA, PI, HIPC, and DMCHBA, while DGA required \(45.7\) steps. This close
grouping is consistent with the relatively small ideal-communication
separations reported in \cite{mybenchmark}. CV showed greater baseline
separation, with R0 means ranging from \(42.8\) to \(50.8\) steps. The paired
results therefore characterize each allocator's sensitivity to restriction
rather than establishing a universal absolute-performance ranking.

Across all six algorithms, reducing the CLIPS candidate set from 100\% to
10\% retention produced a mean paired maximum-step degradation of 8.9\%.
Reducing retention by the remaining nine percentage points, from 10\% to 1\%,
produced an additional 11.4\% degradation. This indicates a large portion of
the candidate set can be removed with comparatively modest performance loss,
whereas further restriction within the final 10\% produces disproportionately
larger degradation. DGA is the principal exception to the otherwise generally
increasing CLIPS trend, while HIPC shows the sharpest low-retention penalty,
reaching 34.5\% degradation at 1\% retention.

CV exhibits a more stable response over its evaluated range of 100\% to 10\%
retention, suggesting that distant tasks have little effect on allocator
performance. CBAA, ACBBA, and PI remain within 3.4\% of unrestricted
performance at 10\% retention, whereas HIPC and DMCHBA exhibit greater
sensitivity as the candidate set is reduced yet remain under 10\% degradation.
DGA once again exhibits non-monotonic behavior. These differences indicate
that both allocator structure and mission-specific candidate semantics
determine how aggressively the candidate set can be restricted before mission
performance deteriorates.

Of the 24,000 primary CLIPS simulation trials, 23,987 completed. The 13
safety-cap failures occurred for HIPC (eight across 50\%, 10\%, 5\%, and 1\%
retention), ACBBA (two at 1\%), PI (two at 1\%), and DGA (one at 50\%).
All 3,000 primary CV trials completed.

\subsection{Effects on Computational Overhead}
\label{sec:compute_effects}

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.99\textwidth]
    {figures/compute_effects_heatmaps.png}
    \caption{Computational effects of value-ranked candidate restriction. Panels
report mean combined candidate-restriction-plus-allocation time per call in
milliseconds for both devices and mission types. Restriction modes are defined
in Table~\ref{tab:restriction_modes}. CAP denotes a condition that
reproducibly exceeded the 30-s per-call execution threshold.}
    \label{fig:compute}
    \vspace{-2mm}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:compute} shows that allocator computational complexity is a
critical consideration for embedded deployment and that candidate restriction
can substantially reduce execution latency. Across the 54 conditions completed
on both platforms, AGX Orin combined allocator-call execution was \(169.9\times\) faster on
average than RP2040 execution, based on the geometric mean of condition-level
speedups. The benefit of restriction, however, depends strongly on allocator
complexity and mission candidate-set size.

The measured restriction-stage share explains part of this dependence. At
10\% retention, candidate discovery, ranking, and truncation accounted for
\(0.4\%\)--\(67.7\%\) of combined AGX time in CLIPS and
\(0.3\%\)--\(63.6\%\) in CV. Among the CLIPS allocators completing at this
setting on the RP2040, the corresponding share was
\(10.8\%\)--\(77.8\%\). Restriction overhead therefore constitutes a large
fraction of execution for inexpensive allocators but remains small relative to
the downstream optimization performed by the most computationally intensive
methods.

In CLIPS, the lower-cost CBAA, ACBBA, and PI allocators initially incur
restriction overhead before reduced allocator workload becomes dominant.
CBAA mean per-call latency more than doubles at 50\% retention relative to
the unrestricted condition, but falls approximately 26\% below R0 at 10\%
retention and 57\% below R0 at 1\%. ACBBA and PI exhibit smaller versions
of the same crossover, reaching reductions of approximately 68\% and 77\%,
respectively, at 10\% retention.

The more computationally intensive HIPC, DMCHBA, and DGA benefit from
restriction over a broader range. At 10\% retention, their AGX mean per-call
latencies are reduced by approximately 81\%, 99.6\%, and 88\%,
respectively, relative to unrestricted execution. DMCHBA shows the strongest
response, decreasing from approximately \(946\)~ms per call at 100\%
retention to \(3.4\)~ms at 10\%.

The RP2040 measurements show when these relative savings become large enough
to change hardware feasibility. In CLIPS, HIPC and DMCHBA exceed the imposed
30-s execution threshold at larger candidate sets but become feasible under
stronger restriction. DMCHBA first completes at 10\% retention with a mean
per-call time of approximately \(1.2\)~s, decreasing to \(40.8\)~ms at 1\%.
HIPC becomes feasible at 25\% retention and falls below \(1\)~s at 5\%.
DGA, despite an approximately 95\% AGX latency reduction between 100\% and
1\% retention, remains above the RP2040 threshold at every tested CLIPS
setting.

CV requires substantially less computation because its initial candidate set
contains 50 targets rather than 361 cells. At 10\% retention, CBAA, ACBBA,
and PI reduce AGX latency by approximately 25\%, 50\%, and 45\%,
respectively, while HIPC, DMCHBA, and DGA achieve reductions of approximately
87\%, 88\%, and 80\%. On the RP2040, DMCHBA becomes feasible at 50\%
retention and decreases to \(96.2\)~ms per call at 10\%, while DGA remains
above the execution threshold through the strongest CV restriction considered.

The CLIPS HIL campaign produced 793 successful system trials. Three additional
trials failed, and 17 of the 48 algorithm--mode conditions were stopped by the
30-s cap. Overall, candidate restriction can both accelerate already-feasible
allocators and move otherwise infeasible algorithms into an executable regime,
but the restriction required to do so is allocator-, mission-, and
hardware-dependent.

