exceeded the imposed per-call timing requirement.

\subsection{Simulator Validation}

HIL execution was first used to evaluate whether moving allocator execution
from the simulator host to the RP2040 introduced systematic mission-performance
bias. Across 793 matched CLIPS trials, the mean paired maximum-step difference
was only $-0.03$ steps. The mean difference was equivalent within $\pm1$ step
(TOST $p=0.0318$), with a 90\% CI of $[-0.89,\,0.83]$ steps. Thus, RP2040
execution preserved the aggregate simulation performance metric without
detectable systematic bias, although individual paired outcomes exhibited
greater variation ($\mathrm{CCC}=0.882$).

Physical execution produced a small but systematic increase in mission cost.
Across 25 matched testbed trials, physical robots required an average of
$2.44$ additional maximum steps (95\% CI: $1.07$--$3.81$, $p=0.0012$).
Despite this bias, simulation and physical outcomes remained highly concordant
($\mathrm{CCC}=0.987$, 95\% CI: $0.964$--$0.995$), and the mean difference
was equivalent within $\pm4$ steps (TOST $p=0.0139$). The physical penalty
was observed across the validated allocator settings rather than being driven
by a single algorithm. These results indicate that simulation captures the
relative physical performance trends with a slight underestimation of embodied
execution cost.

\subsection{Sensitivity to Environment Scale and Communication}

Permutation-based omnibus tests compared the normalized R1--R5
restriction-response curves across environmental conditions. Varying
the CLIPS grid among \(14\times14\), \(19\times19\), and
\(28\times28\) cells produced no significant change in either the
maximum-robot-step or total-team-step curve for any allocator after
Holm correction (all \(p_{\mathrm{Holm}}\geq 0.629\)). Varying the team
among two, four, and eight robots likewise produced no significant
curve differences (all \(p_{\mathrm{Holm}}\geq 0.121\)). Thus, within
the tested ranges, there was no statistically detectable interaction
between candidate restriction and either grid or team size.

Across ideal communication and 25\% Bernoulli, Gilbert--Elliott, and
Rayleigh loss, ACBBA, PI, HIPC, DMCHBA, and DGA also showed no
significant change in either restriction-response curve (all
\(p_{\mathrm{Holm}}\geq 0.397\)). CBAA was the sole exception, with
communication-dependent differences in maximum robot steps
(\(p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=2.4\times10^{-4}\). Follow-up tests localized both effects
to ideal versus Gilbert--Elliott loss (maximum steps:
\(p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=6.0\times10^{-5}\).Overall,
five of the six allocators showed no statistically detectable departure
from the nominal restriction-response pattern outside the
\(19\times19\), four-robot, ideal-communication environment.

\section{Discussion}

\subsubsection{Restriction as a Deployment Tradeoff}

Candidate restriction is most beneficial when candidate-dependent allocation
cost dominates the overhead required to construct the restricted set. HIPC,
DMCHBA, and DGA therefore obtain substantial computational savings over a
broad range, whereas the lighter CBAA, ACBBA, and PI can initially incur
additional overhead in CLIPS before tighter restriction becomes beneficial.
Restriction can nevertheless remain useful for these lighter allocators when
a modest performance penalty is acceptable. Absolute hardware capability is
equally important: large relative reductions did not make DGA feasible on the
RP2040 in CLIPS, while restriction moved HIPC and DMCHBA from capped to
feasible execution. Candidate restriction should therefore be viewed as an
allocator-specific deployment control rather than universally beneficial
preprocessing.

\subsubsection{Allocator and Mission Dependence}

Computation is not determined by candidate count alone. Over the overlapping
low-\(K\) range between mission types, candidate-set size alone explained approximately 1\% of
fixed-effect computation-time variation, compared with about 33\% after
including mission and allocator identity. Significant interactions further
showed that similarly sized candidate sets can impose very different costs
depending on how an allocator processes them. For example, at approximately
\(K=36/38\), HIPC required \(2.20\)~ms per call in CLIPS but \(9.22\)~ms in
CV, while CBAA showed the opposite direction. Thus, neither retained
percentage nor absolute \(K\) provides a portable predictor of execution
latency. Performance also differed by mission: absolute CLIPS maximum-step
performance remained closely grouped across most allocators, whereas CV
produced substantially stronger algorithm separation. The paired percentage metric should consequently be interpreted as
scenario-normalized sensitivity to restriction rather than as the percentage
change between aggregate condition means.

\subsubsection{Algorithm-Specific Behavior}

Strong restriction can alter allocator behavior in addition to reducing
runtime. For HIPC, low-\(K\) CLIPS conditions increase maximum robot steps
more rapidly than total team steps, indicating that restriction increasingly
affects workload balance rather than simply increasing aggregate movement. This suggests HIPC optimizes for reduced team effort by allocating tasks to a smaller number of agents when the task selection pool is tightly restricted.
DGA instead exhibits a non-monotonic response in both missions. This behavior
is consistent with a smaller assignment space allowing a fixed evolutionary
search budget to explore the remaining solutions more effectively, although
the present study does not isolate that mechanism. 

\subsubsection{Deployment Implications}

The validation and sensitivity results support transfer of the observed
tradeoffs beyond the nominal simulator condition. RP2040 HIL execution showed
negligible aggregate performance bias, while physical trials preserved high
simulation concordance with a small step execution penalty. Restriction-response curves were also stable
across the tested grid and team sizes and across communication conditions for
five of the six allocators. Collectively, these results indicate that the
appropriate candidate limit is not a universal retained fraction, or even a
universal absolute \(K\), but an allocator--mission--hardware operating
parameter. A natural extension is therefore adaptive candidate restriction
based on measured execution latency and mission state.

\subsubsection{Limitations}

This study is limited to the two mission types, six decentralized allocators,
and two embedded platforms considered here, and the observed tradeoffs should
not be assumed to generalize to all MRTA problems or robotic hardware.
This scope was selected to enable controlled comparisons across distinct
missions, allocator families, and computational platforms. Additionally, only
one allocator-independent candidate-restriction mechanism is evaluated,
without direct comparison to algorithm-specific or application-specific
reduction methods from prior work. This design is reasonable for the present
objective because a common restriction mechanism isolates differences in how
allocators respond to candidate-set reduction without introducing
algorithm-specific modifications. Evaluation depth also differs by mission, with CLIPS serving as the primary campaign through a larger paired simulation study and physical testbed validation, while CV provides a complementary cross-mission evaluation with fewer paired trials and no physical testbed validation. Future work should extend this framework to
additional missions, hardware platforms, ranking strategies, specialized
restriction methods, and adaptive restriction mechanisms.

\section{Conclusion}

This study introduced a uniform, allocator-independent, value-ranked
Top-\(K\) candidate-restriction mechanism and provided a cross-family
characterization across six decentralized allocators, two mission structures,
and AGX Orin and RP2040 platforms. Timing decomposition showed that
restriction overhead can dominate inexpensive allocators, whereas reducing the
admitted task set provides broad savings for methods with greater
candidate-dependent computation. At 10\% CLIPS retention, AGX per-call
latency decreased by approximately 26\%--99.6\% across algorithms with an
8.9\% mean paired maximum-step penalty. Restriction also moved HIPC and
DMCHBA into an RP2040-feasible regime, while DGA remained infeasible under
the imposed threshold. Cross-mission, sensitivity, HIL, and physical results
showed that useful operating regions transfer beyond the nominal simulation
but are not predicted by retained percentage or absolute \(K\) alone.
Candidate limit should therefore be treated as an
allocator--mission--hardware deployment parameter, motivating future adaptive
selection based on online mission state and measured execution latency.



